========================================================================================================================
                              VIRSH / VIRT-INSTALL: DEPLOYING NEW VIRTUAL MACHINES
========================================================================================================================

SCENARIO 1: SILENT / UNATTENDED INSTALLATION FROM ISO
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
To perform a completely silent installation, we use `virt-install` with an answer file (like a Kickstart file for 
RedHat/CentOS/Rocky or a Preseed file for Debian/Ubuntu).

Command  | virt-install \
         |   --name production-vm \
         |   --vcpus 2 \
         |   --ram 2048 \
         |   --location /var/lib/libvirt/images/Rocky-9-minimal.iso \
         |   --disk size=20,pool=default,format=qcow2 \
         |   --network network=default,model=virtio \
         |   --graphics none \
         |   --console pty,target_type=serial \
         |   --extra-args "inst.ks=http://192.168.122 console=ttyS0,115200n8" \
         |   --noautoconsole
         |
Fields   | --location    : Replaces --cdrom. Required so libvirt can inject kernel extra-args.
         | --graphics none: Disables VNC/SPICE graphical displays.
         | --extra-args  : Feeds the automation script URL (inst.ks) and redirects output to the serial console.
         | --noautoconsole: Returns control back to your terminal host immediately while installation runs silently.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SCENARIO 2: CREATING A VM USING AN EXISTING QCOW2 DISK IMAGE
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Use this method when you already have a pre-configured or cloud-init image (like an Ubuntu Cloud Image) and want to spin 
up a VM instantly without going through an installation setup wizard.

Step 1   | Copy your existing qcow2 template to the libvirt storage directory:
         | cp /tmp/template.qcow2 /var/lib/libvirt/images/cloned-vm.qcow2
         |
Step 2   | Provision the VM using the copied disk as the primary storage layout:
         | virt-install \
         |   --name app-server-vm \
         |   --vcpus 2 \
         |   --ram 4096 \
         |   --disk path=/var/lib/libvirt/images/cloned-vm.qcow2,format=qcow2,bus=virtio \
         |   --network network=default,model=virtio \
         |   --os-variant ubuntu22.04 \
         |   --import \
         |   --noautoconsole
         |
Fields   | --disk path=  : Points directly to your existing, copied qcow2 image.
         | --os-variant  : Optimizes CPU/kernel settings for the specified OS profile.
         | --import      : Tells libvirt to skip any OS installation process and boot straight from the disk.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
POST-DEPLOYMENT MANAGEMENT COMMANDS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Action                   | Command
-------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
View installation status | virsh list --all
Connect to text console  | virsh console production-vm  (Press Ctrl + ] to exit the console)
Force stop the VM        | virsh destroy production-vm
Completely delete VM     | virsh undefine app-server-vm --remove-all-storage
